# Workflow steps

## Setup

1. Create a free account on usegalaxy.eu. Galaxy runs on three main servers (US, Europe, Australia) and the tools and workflows differ between them. The workshop used the European one because the workflows were already available there.
2. Create one history per stage and rename it: Sequence QC, Assembly, Annotation.
3. Load the data into the history by URL (see data/README_data.md).
4. Import the workflows: Workflows, Import, search the GA4GH servers for "Large Genome Assembly". The ones used were Data QC, kmer counting - meryl, Trim and filter reads - fastp, Assembly with Flye, Assembly Polishing and Assess Genome Quality.
5. Change the datatype of the three Eucalyptus read files (R1, R2 and nanopore) to fastqsanger.gz before running the workflows. Pencil icon, Datatypes tab, type the new datatype and save. Some tools fail if this is skipped (see troubleshooting).

## Step 1. Read quality control

Purpose: decide whether the reads can be used before spending time on assembly. Poor reads give a poor assembly however good the later tools are.

Tools: FastQC for Illumina reads, NanoPlot for long reads, MultiQC to merge the FastQC reports.

Settings: I left the optional contaminant and adapter lists empty and kept every module active. Other options stayed at their defaults, as in the demo.

What to look at:
- basic statistics: number of reads, read length, %GC
- per base quality: a sudden drop is a warning; a small decline at the end is normal
- per tile quality: flow cell problems
- per base sequence content, GC distribution, duplication levels, adapter content, overrepresented sequences
- NanoPlot: mean read length, mean quality, and the plot of read length against quality

The workshop first compared a 16S amplicon file with an Illumina genome run to show what a failed QC looks like. In the amplicon file quality fell sharply after roughly 110 bases and adapters were present. Amplicon data also show base composition bias and many duplicates because the same gene is sequenced over and over; that is expected and not a sequencing fault.

## Step 2. k-mer counting

Workflow: kmer counting - meryl. Input: the Illumina forward reads (R1) only.

Meryl counts k-mers of different sizes and GenomeScope fits a model to the histogram. The output gives an estimate of genome size, heterozygosity, the length of repeated sequence and the length of unique sequence, plus a plot of coverage against frequency.

How to read the plot: very short, very frequent k-mers at low coverage are errors. The main peak is the homozygous sequence. A smaller peak at roughly half that coverage comes from heterozygous regions. Repeats show up at high coverage.

Caution: the data are a subsample, so the values describe the subsample and not the real genome.

## Step 3. Trimming and filtering

Workflow: Trim and filter reads - fastp, run on R1, R2 and the long reads.

- Illumina: bases below Phred 15 are trimmed and very short reads are discarded (workflow defaults)
- Long reads: no quality trimming on purpose, because the short reads supply the accuracy and losing long reads costs contiguity

Result in the demo: 3.21 million reads before, 3.20 million after, so under 1% removed. The reads were already good, so trimming changed very little. The parameters can be edited in the workflow editor.

